97
00:04:29,840 --> 00:04:33,440
So this is computer code almost for these web pages.

98
00:04:33,440 --> 00:04:35,720
But what we really want is we just want this text, right?

99
00:04:35,720 --> 00:04:37,760
We just want the text of this web page

100
00:04:37,760 --> 00:04:40,000
and we don't want the navigation and things like that.

101
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:42,120
So there's a lot of filtering and processing

102
00:04:42,120 --> 00:04:45,040
and heuristics that go into adequately filtering

103
00:04:45,040 --> 00:04:49,360
for just the good content of these web pages.

104
00:04:49,360 --> 00:04:51,640
The next stage here is language filtering.

105
00:04:51,640 --> 00:04:56,400
So for example, a fine web filters using a language classifier.

106
00:04:56,400 --> 00:04:59,960
They try to guess what language every single web page is in.

107
00:04:59,960 --> 00:05:01,360
And then they only keep web pages that

108
00:05:01,360 --> 00:05:05,190
have more than 65% of English as an example.

109
00:05:05,190 --> 00:05:06,310
And so you can get a sense that this is

110
00:05:06,310 --> 00:05:10,230
like a design decision that different companies can take

111
00:05:10,230 --> 00:05:11,350
for themselves.

112
00:05:11,350 --> 00:05:13,870
What fraction of all different types of languages

113
00:05:13,870 --> 00:05:15,710
are we going to include in our data set?

114
00:05:15,710 --> 00:05:17,310
Because for example, if we filter out

115
00:05:17,310 --> 00:05:19,190
all of the Spanish as an example, then

116
00:05:19,190 --> 00:05:22,470
You might imagine that our model later will not be very good at Spanish because it's just

117
00:05:22,470 --> 00:05:25,550
never seen that much data of that language.

118
00:05:25,550 --> 00:05:29,750
And so different companies can focus on multi-langual performance to a different degree

119
00:05:29,750 --> 00:05:30,830
as an example.

120
00:05:30,830 --> 00:05:34,950
So fine web is quite focused on English, and so their language model, if they end up

121
00:05:34,950 --> 00:05:40,920
training one later, will be very good at English, but not maybe very good at other languages.

122
00:05:40,920 --> 00:05:44,600
After language filtering, there's a few other filtering steps and deduplication and things

123
00:05:44,600 --> 00:05:49,600
like that, finishing with, for example, the PII removal.

124
00:05:49,880 --> 00:05:53,080
This is personally identifiable information.

125
00:05:53,080 --> 00:05:55,480
So as an example, addresses, social security numbers,

126
00:05:55,480 --> 00:05:57,600
and things like that, you would try to detect them

127
00:05:57,600 --> 00:05:59,480
and you would try to filter out those kinds of web pages

128
00:05:59,480 --> 00:06:01,160
from the data set as well.

129
00:06:01,160 --> 00:06:02,480
So there's a lot of stages here

130
00:06:02,480 --> 00:06:04,200
and I won't go into full detail,

131
00:06:04,200 --> 00:06:07,560
but it is a fairly extensive part of the pre-processing

132
00:06:07,560 --> 00:06:10,560
and you end up with, for example, the fine web data set.

133
00:06:10,560 --> 00:06:13,320
So when you click in on it, you can see some examples here

134
00:06:13,320 --> 00:06:15,040
of what this actually ends up looking like,

135
00:06:15,040 --> 00:06:19,070
and anyone can download this on the Hanging Face webpage.

136
00:06:19,070 --> 00:06:21,350
And so here's some examples of the final text

137
00:06:21,350 --> 00:06:23,380
that ends up in the training set.

138
00:06:23,380 --> 00:06:27,660
So this is some article about tornadoes in 2012.

139
00:06:28,940 --> 00:06:31,700
So there's some tornadoes in 2020 and 2012

140
00:06:31,700 --> 00:06:32,620
and what happened.

