00:06:42,260 --> 00:06:47,870
Okay, so this is kind of an odd medical article.

145
00:06:47,870 --> 00:06:50,390
So just think of these as basically

146
00:06:50,390 --> 00:06:53,230
web pages on the internet filtered just for the text

147
00:06:53,230 --> 00:06:54,310
in various ways.

148
00:06:54,310 --> 00:06:57,870
And now we have a ton of text, 40 terabytes of it.

149
00:06:57,870 --> 00:06:59,470
And that now is the starting point

150
00:06:59,470 --> 00:07:01,790
for the next step of this stage.

151
00:07:01,790 --> 00:07:03,390
Now I wanted to give you an intuitive sense

152
00:07:03,390 --> 00:07:04,630
of where we are right now.

153
00:07:04,630 --> 00:07:06,950
So I took the first 200 web pages here

154
00:07:06,950 --> 00:07:09,150
and remember we have tons of them.

155
00:07:09,150 --> 00:07:10,710
And I just take all that text

156
00:07:10,710 --> 00:07:13,430
and I just put it all together, concatenated.

157
00:07:13,430 --> 00:07:14,710
And so this is what we end up with.

158
00:07:14,710 --> 00:07:19,700
We just get this just raw text, raw internet text.

159
00:07:19,700 --> 00:07:21,940
And there's a ton of it, even in these 200 web pages.

160
00:07:21,940 --> 00:07:24,180
So I can continue zooming out here.

161
00:07:24,180 --> 00:07:28,020
And we just have this massive tapestry of text data.

162
00:07:28,020 --> 00:07:30,220
And this text data has all these patterns.

163
00:07:30,220 --> 00:07:31,540
And what we want to do now is we want

164
00:07:31,540 --> 00:07:33,980
to start training neural networks on this data.

165
00:07:33,980 --> 00:07:37,920
So the neural hours can internalize and model

166
00:07:37,920 --> 00:07:41,120
how this text flows, right?

167
00:07:41,120 --> 00:07:44,120
So we just have this giant texture of text

168
00:07:44,120 --> 00:07:48,010
and now we want to get neural lots that mimic it.

169
00:07:48,010 --> 00:07:51,330
Okay, now before we plug text into neural networks,

170
00:07:51,330 --> 00:07:54,290
we have to decide how we're going to represent this text

171
00:07:54,290 --> 00:07:56,210
and how we're gonna feed it in.

172
00:07:56,210 --> 00:07:58,610
Now, the way our technology works for these neural lots

173
00:07:58,610 --> 00:08:02,810
is that they expect a one-dimensional sequence of symbols

174
00:08:02,810 --> 00:08:07,530
and they want a finite set of symbols that are possible.

175
00:08:07,530 --> 00:08:09,890
And so we have to decide what are the symbols

176
00:08:09,890 --> 00:08:11,570
and then we have to represent our data

177
00:08:11,570 --> 00:08:15,230
as one dimensional sequence of those symbols.

178
00:08:15,230 --> 00:08:17,470
So right now what we have is a one dimensional sequence

179
00:08:17,470 --> 00:08:18,430
of text.

180
00:08:18,430 --> 00:08:21,670
It starts here and it goes here and then it comes here, etc.

181
00:08:21,670 --> 00:08:23,390
So this is one dimensional sequence

182
00:08:23,390 --> 00:08:24,790
even though on my monitor, of course,

183
00:08:24,790 --> 00:08:27,230
it's laid out in a two dimensional way

184
00:08:27,230 --> 00:08:29,710
but it goes from left to right and top to bottom, right?

185
00:08:29,710 --> 00:08:32,190
So it's a one dimensional sequence of text.

186
00:08:32,190 --> 00:08:33,950
Now this being computers, of course,

187
00:08:33,950 --> 00:08:35,990
there's an underlying representation here.

188
00:08:35,990 --> 00:08:39,810
So if I do what's called UTF-8 encode this text,

189
00:08:39,810 --> 00:08:43,050
then I can get the raw bits that correspond

190
00:08:43,050 --> 00:08:45,190
to this text in the computer.

191
00:08:45,190 --> 00:08:48,040
And that looks like this.

192
00:08:48,040 --> 00:08:50,560
So it turns out that, for example,

193
00:08:50,560 --> 00:08:55,080
this very first bar here is the first eight bits

194
00:08:55,080 --> 00:08:57,100
as an example.

195
00:08:57,100 --> 00:08:58,620
So what is this thing, right?

196
00:08:58,620 --> 00:09:02,500
This is a representation that we are looking for

197
00:09:02,500 --> 00:09:04,180
in the circumstance.

198
00:09:04,180 --> 00:09:06,300
We have exactly two possible symbols,

199
00:09:06,300 --> 00:09:11,100
zero and one, and we have a very long sequence of it, right?

200
00:09:11,980 --> 00:09:17,500
Now, as it turns out, this sequence length is actually going to be very finite and

201
00:09:17,500 --> 00:09:22,540
precious resource in our neural network, and we actually don't want extremely long sequences

202
00:09:22,540 --> 00:09:30,220
of just two symbols. Instead, what we want is we want to trade off this symbol size

203
00:09:30,940 --> 00:09:35,420
of this vocabulary, as we call it, and the resulting sequence length. So, we don't want

204
00:09:35,420 --> 00:09:41,420
just two symbols and extremely long sequences, we're going to want more symbols and shorter sequences.

205
00:09:41,900 --> 00:09:47,660
Okay, so one naive way of compressing or decreasing the length of our sequence here is to basically

206
00:09:48,380 --> 00:09:54,860
consider some group of consecutive bits, for example, eight bits and group them into a single

207
00:09:54,860 --> 00:10:01,060
what's called byte. So because these bits are either on or off, if we take a group of eight of them,

208
00:10:01,060 --> 00:10:05,380
there turns out to be only 256 possible combinations of how these bits could be honor off.

209
00:10:06,020 --> 00:10:10,660
And so therefore we can re-represent this sequence into a sequence of bytes instead.

210
00:10:11,500 --> 00:10:18,300
So this sequence of bytes will be 8 times shorter, but now we have 256 possible symbols.

211
00:10:18,300 --> 00:10:20,860
So every number here goes from 0 to 255.

212
00:10:21,670 --> 00:10:27,270
Now I really encourage you to think of these not as numbers, but as unique IDs or like unique symbols.

213
00:10:27,270 --> 00:10:33,270
So, maybe it's a bit more, maybe it's better to actually think of these, to replace every one of these with a unique emoji.

214
00:10:33,270 --> 00:10:35,270
You'd get something like this.

215
00:10:35,270 --> 00:10:42,750
So, we basically have a sequence of emojis and there's 256 possible emojis. You can think of it that way.

216
00:10:42,750 --> 00:10:48,850
Now, it turns out that in production for state-of-the-art language models, you actually want to go even beyond this.

217
00:10:48,850 --> 00:10:57,850
He wants to continue to shrink the length of the sequence because again it is a precious resource in return for more symbols in your vocabulary.

218
00:10:57,850 --> 00:11:03,850
And the way this is done is done by running what's called the byte pair encoding algorithm.

219
00:11:03,850 --> 00:11:11,850
And the way this works is we're basically looking for consecutive bytes or symbols that are very common.

220
00:11:11,850 --> 00:11:17,890
So for example, it turns out that the sequence 116 followed by 32 is quite common and occurs

221
00:11:17,890 --> 00:11:19,210
very frequently.

222
00:11:19,210 --> 00:11:25,530
So what we're going to do is we're going to group this pair into a new symbol.

223
00:11:25,530 --> 00:11:30,870
So we're going to mint a symbol with an ID256 and we're going to rewrite every single

224
00:11:30,870 --> 00:11:33,930
pair 116 and 32 with this new symbol.

225
00:11:33,930 --> 00:11:37,170
And then we can iterate this algorithm as many times as we wish.

226
00:11:37,170 --> 00:11:41,370
And each time when we mint a new symbol, we're decreasing the length and we're increasing

227
00:11:41,370 --> 00:11:47,850
the symbol size. And in practice it turns out that a pretty good setting of the basically

228
00:11:47,850 --> 00:11:54,010
the vocabulary size turns out to be about 100,000 possible symbols. So in particular GPT-4

229
00:11:54,010 --> 00:12:06,700
uses 100,000 and 277 symbols. And this process of converting from raw text into these symbols

230
00:12:06,700 --> 00:12:11,420
or as we call them tokens, is the process called tokenization.

231
00:12:11,420 --> 00:12:15,100
So let's now take a look at how GPT-4 performs tokenization,

232
00:12:15,100 --> 00:12:19,100
converting from text to tokens and from tokens back to text,

233
00:12:19,100 --> 00:12:20,420
and what this actually looks like.

234
00:12:20,420 --> 00:12:22,300
So one website I like to use to explore

235
00:12:22,300 --> 00:12:26,620
these token representations is called tick tokenizer.

236
00:12:26,620 --> 00:12:30,140
And so come here to the dropdown and select CL100K base,

237
00:12:30,140 --> 00:12:33,380
which is the GPT-4 base model tokenizer.

238
00:12:33,380 --> 00:12:35,180
And here on the left, you can put in text,

239
00:12:35,180 --> 00:12:37,400
And it shows you the tokenization of that text.

240
00:12:37,400 --> 00:12:42,910
So for example, have low space world.

241
00:12:44,810 --> 00:12:47,730
So hello world turns out to be exactly two tokens.

242
00:12:47,730 --> 00:12:52,730
The token hello, which is the token with ID 15339

243
00:12:52,730 --> 00:12:57,890
and the token space world, that is the token 1917.

244
00:12:58,780 --> 00:13:02,060
So hello space world.

245
00:13:02,060 --> 00:13:04,620
Now if I was to join these two for example,

