So we sample from the distribution

488
00:27:06,480 --> 00:27:09,080
to get a single unique token.

489
00:27:09,080 --> 00:27:12,580
So for example, token 860 comes next.

490
00:27:12,580 --> 00:27:14,500
So 860 in this case, when we're generating

491
00:27:14,500 --> 00:27:16,180
from model could come next.

492
00:27:16,180 --> 00:27:18,620
Now, 860 is a relatively likely token.

493
00:27:18,620 --> 00:27:21,060
It might not be the only possible token in this case.

494
00:27:21,060 --> 00:27:22,060
There could be many other tokens

495
00:27:22,060 --> 00:27:23,460
that could have been sampled.

496
00:27:23,460 --> 00:27:25,980
But we could see that 860 is a relatively likely token

497
00:27:25,980 --> 00:27:29,140
as an example, and indeed in our training example here,

498
00:27:29,140 --> 00:27:31,500
860 does follow 91.

499
00:27:31,500 --> 00:27:35,570
So let's not say that we continue the process.

500
00:27:35,570 --> 00:27:38,330
So after 91, there's a 60, we append it,

501
00:27:38,330 --> 00:27:41,290
and we again ask, what is the third token?

502
00:27:41,290 --> 00:27:43,650
Let's sample, and let's just say that it's 287.

503
00:27:43,650 --> 00:27:45,670
Exactly as here.

504
00:27:45,670 --> 00:27:46,750
Let's do that again.

505
00:27:46,750 --> 00:27:47,990
We come back in.

506
00:27:47,990 --> 00:27:50,310
Now we have a sequence of three, and we ask,

507
00:27:50,310 --> 00:27:52,390
what is the likely fourth token?

508
00:27:52,390 --> 00:27:55,560
And we sample from that and get this one.

509
00:27:55,560 --> 00:27:58,070
And now let's see we do it one more time.

510
00:27:58,070 --> 00:28:01,510
We take those four, we sample, and we get this one.

511
00:28:01,510 --> 00:28:06,750
And this 13659, this is not actually 3962

512
00:28:06,750 --> 00:28:08,030
as we had before.

513
00:28:08,030 --> 00:28:11,470
So this token is the token article, instead.

514
00:28:11,470 --> 00:28:13,860
So viewing a single article.

515
00:28:13,860 --> 00:28:16,580
And so in this case, we didn't exactly reproduce

516
00:28:16,580 --> 00:28:19,860
the sequence that we saw here in the training data.

517
00:28:19,860 --> 00:28:22,580
So keep in mind that these systems are stochastic.

518
00:28:22,580 --> 00:28:26,180
They have, we're sampling, and we're flipping

519
00:28:26,180 --> 00:28:31,620
coins and sometimes we lock out and we reproduce some like small chunk of the text and

520
00:28:31,620 --> 00:28:37,780
training set, but sometimes we're getting a token that was not verbatim part of any

521
00:28:37,780 --> 00:28:43,380
of the documents in the training data. So we're going to get sort of like remixes of the data

522
00:28:43,380 --> 00:28:47,540
that we saw in the training because at every step of the way, we can flip and get a slightly

523
00:28:47,540 --> 00:28:52,100
different token and then once that token makes it in, if you sample the next one and so on,

524
00:28:52,100 --> 00:29:00,100
You very quickly start to generate token streams that are very different from the token streams that occur in the training documents.

525
00:29:00,100 --> 00:29:06,100
So statistically, they will have similar properties, but they are not identical to training data.

526
00:29:06,100 --> 00:29:08,100
They're kind of like inspired by the training data.

527
00:29:08,100 --> 00:29:11,100
And so in this case, we got a slightly different sequence.

528
00:29:11,100 --> 00:29:13,100
And why would we get article?

529
00:29:13,100 --> 00:29:20,100
You might imagine that article is a relatively likely token in the context of bar viewing, single, etc.

530
00:29:20,100 --> 00:29:22,300
and you could imagine that the word article

531
00:29:22,300 --> 00:29:24,380
followed this context window,

532
00:29:24,380 --> 00:29:27,620
somewhere in the training documents to some extent

533
00:29:27,620 --> 00:29:30,310
and we just happened to sample it here at that stage.

534
00:29:30,310 --> 00:29:33,270
So basically inference is just predicting

535
00:29:33,270 --> 00:29:35,270
from these distributions one at a time,

536
00:29:35,270 --> 00:29:38,350
we continue feeding back tokens and getting the next one.

537
00:29:38,350 --> 00:29:41,310
And we're always flipping these coins

538
00:29:41,310 --> 00:29:43,510
and depending on how lucky or unlucky we get,

539
00:29:45,180 --> 00:29:46,980
we might get very different kinds of patterns

540
00:29:46,980 --> 00:29:49,100
depending on how we sample

541
00:29:49,100 --> 00:29:51,300
from these probability distributions.

542
00:29:51,300 --> 00:29:52,380
So that's inference.

543
00:29:52,380 --> 00:29:54,180
So in most common scenarios,

544
00:29:55,140 --> 00:29:57,180
basically downloading the internet

545
00:29:57,180 --> 00:29:59,140
and tokenizing it as a pre-processing step,

546
00:29:59,140 --> 00:30:01,140
you do that a single time.

547
00:30:01,140 --> 00:30:04,220
And then once you have your token sequence,

548
00:30:04,220 --> 00:30:06,060
we can start training networks.

549
00:30:06,060 --> 00:30:07,540
And in practical cases,

550
00:30:07,540 --> 00:30:09,660
you would try to train many different networks

551
00:30:09,660 --> 00:30:11,460
of different kinds of settings

552
00:30:11,460 --> 00:30:12,780
and different kinds of arrangements

553
00:30:12,780 --> 00:30:14,460
and different kinds of sizes.

554
00:30:14,460 --> 00:30:16,940
And so you'd be doing a lot of neural network training

555
00:30:16,940 --> 00:30:20,100
And then once you have a neural network and you train it,

556
00:30:20,100 --> 00:30:22,100
and you have some specific set of parameters

557
00:30:22,100 --> 00:30:25,440
that you're happy with, then you can take the model

558
00:30:25,440 --> 00:30:26,760
and you can do inference.

559
00:30:26,760 --> 00:30:29,960
And you can actually generate data from the model.

560
00:30:29,960 --> 00:30:31,600
And when you're on charge GPT and you're talking

561
00:30:31,600 --> 00:30:33,840
with the model, that model is trained

562
00:30:33,840 --> 00:30:37,920
and has been trained by OpenAI many months ago, probably.

563
00:30:37,920 --> 00:30:41,400
And they have a specific set of weights that work well.

564
00:30:41,400 --> 00:30:42,720
And when you're talking to the model,

565
00:30:42,720 --> 00:30:44,320
all of that is just inference.

566
00:30:44,320 --> 00:30:45,720
There's no more training.

567
00:30:45,720 --> 00:30:47,920
those parameters are held fixed,

568
00:30:47,920 --> 00:30:50,920
and you're just talking to the model sort of.

569
00:30:50,920 --> 00:30:52,560
You're giving it some of the tokens,

570
00:30:52,560 --> 00:30:54,680
and it's kind of completing token sequences.

571
00:30:54,680 --> 00:30:56,920
And that's what you're seeing generated

572
00:30:56,920 --> 00:30:59,280
when you actually use the model on chat GPT.

573
00:30:59,280 --> 00:31:02,240
So that model then just does inference alone.

574
00:31:02,240 --> 00:31:04,680
So let's now look at an example of training an inference

575
00:31:04,680 --> 00:31:06,000
that is kind of concrete,

576
00:31:06,000 --> 00:31:08,240
and gives you a sense of what this actually looks like

577
00:31:08,240 --> 00:31:09,840
when these models are trained.

578
00:31:09,840 --> 00:31:11,760
Now the example that I would like to work with

579
00:31:11,760 --> 00:31:13,600
and that I'm particularly fond of

580
00:31:13,600 --> 00:31:15,840
is that of OpenAI's GPT-2.

581
00:31:15,840 --> 00:31:19,480
So GPT stands for generically pre-trained transformer,

582
00:31:19,480 --> 00:31:20,800
and this is the second iteration

583
00:31:20,800 --> 00:31:23,420
of the GPT series by OpenAI.

