00:51:20,520 --> 00:51:22,960
to be remembered correctly compared to things

987
00:51:22,960 --> 00:51:24,960
that have happened very infrequently.

988
00:51:24,960 --> 00:51:26,560
So you can't fully trust some of the things

989
00:51:26,560 --> 00:51:28,240
that is some of the information that is here

990
00:51:28,240 --> 00:51:30,240
because it's all just a vague recollection

991
00:51:30,240 --> 00:51:32,040
of internet documents.

992
00:51:32,040 --> 00:51:34,800
Because the information is not stored explicitly

993
00:51:34,800 --> 00:51:35,840
in any of the parameters.

994
00:51:35,840 --> 00:51:37,920
It's all just the recollection.

995
00:51:37,920 --> 00:51:38,920
That's that we did get something

996
00:51:38,920 --> 00:51:40,760
that is probably approximately correct.

997
00:51:40,760 --> 00:51:43,360
And I don't actually have the expertise to verify

998
00:51:43,360 --> 00:51:44,880
that this is roughly correct.

999
00:51:44,880 --> 00:51:46,400
But you see that we've elicited

1000
00:51:46,400 --> 00:51:48,840
a lot of the knowledge of the model.

1001
00:51:48,840 --> 00:51:51,400
And this knowledge is not precise and exact.

1002
00:51:51,400 --> 00:51:55,600
This knowledge is vague and probabilistic and statistical.

1003
00:51:55,600 --> 00:51:57,600
And the kinds of things that occur often

1004
00:51:57,600 --> 00:51:59,040
are the kinds of things that are more likely

1005
00:51:59,040 --> 00:52:02,200
to be remembered in the model.

1006
00:52:02,200 --> 00:52:03,840
Now, I want to show you a few more examples

1007
00:52:03,840 --> 00:52:05,400
of this model's behavior.

1008
00:52:05,400 --> 00:52:06,400
The first thing I want to show you

1009
00:52:06,400 --> 00:52:08,120
is this example.

1010
00:52:08,120 --> 00:52:10,280
I went to the Wikipedia page for Zebra,

1011
00:52:10,280 --> 00:52:15,640
and let me just copy paste the first even one sentence here.

1012
00:52:15,640 --> 00:52:17,120
And let me put it here.

1013
00:52:17,120 --> 00:52:20,000
Now, when I click Enter, what kind of completion

1014
00:52:20,000 --> 00:52:21,690
are we going to get?

1015
00:52:21,690 --> 00:52:24,520
So let me just hit Enter.

1016
00:52:24,520 --> 00:52:28,200
There are three living species, et cetera, et cetera.

1017
00:52:28,200 --> 00:52:31,440
What the model is producing here is an exact regurgitation

1018
00:52:31,440 --> 00:52:32,640
of this Wikipedia entry.

1019
00:52:32,640 --> 00:52:36,360
It is reciting this Wikipedia entry purely from memory

1020
00:52:36,360 --> 00:52:39,160
and this memory is stored in its parameters.

1021
00:52:39,160 --> 00:52:41,440
And so it is possible that at some point

1022
00:52:41,440 --> 00:52:44,960
in these 512 tokens, the model will stray away

1023
00:52:44,960 --> 00:52:46,440
from the Wikipedia entry.

1024
00:52:46,440 --> 00:52:49,520
But you can see that it has huge chunks of it memorized here.

1025
00:52:49,520 --> 00:52:53,810
Let me see, for example, if this sentence occurs by now.

1026
00:52:53,810 --> 00:52:56,650
OK, so we're still on track.

1027
00:52:56,650 --> 00:52:59,580
Let me check here.

1028
00:52:59,580 --> 00:53:01,870
OK, we're still on track.

1029
00:53:01,870 --> 00:53:05,460
it will eventually stray away.

1030
00:53:05,460 --> 00:53:07,100
Okay, so this thing is just to recite it

1031
00:53:07,100 --> 00:53:08,420
to a very large extent.

1032
00:53:08,420 --> 00:53:10,620
It will eventually deviate

1033
00:53:10,620 --> 00:53:12,860
because it won't be able to remember exactly.

1034
00:53:12,860 --> 00:53:14,060
Now, the reason that this happens

1035
00:53:14,060 --> 00:53:17,580
is because these models can be extremely good at memorization.

1036
00:53:17,580 --> 00:53:20,220
And usually this is not what you want in the final model,

1037
00:53:20,220 --> 00:53:21,860
and this is something called recursion,

1038
00:53:21,860 --> 00:53:25,420
and it's usually undesirable to cite things

1039
00:53:25,420 --> 00:53:28,110
directly that you have trained on.

1040
00:53:28,110 --> 00:53:29,870
Now, the reason that this happens actually

1041
00:53:29,870 --> 00:53:31,990
is because for a lot of documents,

1042
00:53:31,990 --> 00:53:36,790
Like, for example, Wikipedia, when these documents are deemed to be of very high quality as a source,

1043
00:53:36,790 --> 00:53:41,830
like, for example, Wikipedia, it is very often the case that when you train the model,

1044
00:53:41,830 --> 00:53:45,270
you will preferentially sample from those sources.

1045
00:53:45,270 --> 00:53:48,630
So basically, the model has probably done a few epochs on this data,

1046
00:53:48,630 --> 00:53:52,550
meaning that it has seen this web page, like maybe probably 10 times or so.

1047
00:53:52,550 --> 00:53:56,230
And it's a bit like you, like when you read some kind of a text many, many times,

1048
00:53:56,230 --> 00:53:59,830
say you read something a hundred times, then you will be able to recite it.

1049
00:53:59,830 --> 00:54:01,390
And it's very similar for this model.

1050
00:54:01,390 --> 00:54:02,950
If it sees something way too often,

1051
00:54:02,950 --> 00:54:05,950
it's going to be able to recite it later from memory.

1052
00:54:05,950 --> 00:54:09,270
Except these models can be a lot more efficient

1053
00:54:09,270 --> 00:54:11,150
at like per presentation than a human.

1054
00:54:11,150 --> 00:54:13,510
So probably it's only seen this Wikipedia entry 10 times,

1055
00:54:13,510 --> 00:54:15,710
but basically it has remembered this article

1056
00:54:15,710 --> 00:54:17,710
exactly in its parameters.

1057
00:54:17,710 --> 00:54:18,870
OK, the next thing I'm going to show you

1058
00:54:18,870 --> 00:54:21,150
is something that the model has definitely not seen

1059
00:54:21,150 --> 00:54:22,630
during its training.

1060
00:54:22,630 --> 00:54:25,550
So for example, if we go to the paper

1061
00:54:25,550 --> 00:54:28,400
and then we navigate to the pre-training data,

1062
00:54:28,400 --> 00:54:33,390
We'll see here that the dataset has a knowledge cutoff

1063
00:54:33,390 --> 00:54:35,230
until the end of 2023.

1064
00:54:35,230 --> 00:54:38,550
So it will not have seen documents after this point.

1065
00:54:38,550 --> 00:54:39,870
And certainly it has not seen anything

1066
00:54:39,870 --> 00:54:42,990
about the 2024 election and how it turned out.

1067
00:54:42,990 --> 00:54:48,030
Now, if we prime the model with the tokens from the future,

1068
00:54:48,030 --> 00:54:49,990
it will continue the token sequence

1069
00:54:49,990 --> 00:54:51,390
and it will just take its best guess,

1070
00:54:51,390 --> 00:54:54,390
according to the knowledge that it has in its own parameters.

1071
00:54:54,390 --> 00:54:57,000
So let's take a look at what that could look like.

1072
00:54:57,000 --> 00:55:00,610
So the Republican Party could Trump,

1073
00:55:00,610 --> 00:55:04,010
okay, President of the United States from 2017,

1074
00:55:04,010 --> 00:55:06,010
and let's see what it says after this point.

1075
00:55:06,010 --> 00:55:07,970
So for example, the model will have to guess

1076
00:55:07,970 --> 00:55:10,650
at the running mate and who it's against, et cetera.

1077
00:55:10,650 --> 00:55:12,830
So let's hit enter.

1078
00:55:12,830 --> 00:55:15,270
So here are things that Mike Pence was the running mate

1079
00:55:15,270 --> 00:55:17,310
instead of JD Ves.

1080
00:55:17,310 --> 00:55:22,310
And the ticket was against Hillary Clinton and Tim Cain.

1081
00:55:22,710 --> 00:55:25,230
So this is kind of an interesting parallel universe

1082
00:55:25,230 --> 00:55:26,550
potentially of what could have happened

1083
00:55:26,550 --> 00:55:28,070
according to the alarm.

1084
00:55:28,070 --> 00:55:29,750
Let's get a different sample.

1085
00:55:29,750 --> 00:55:34,540
So the identical prompt and let's re-sample.

1086
00:55:34,540 --> 00:55:37,060
So here the running mate was Ron DeSantis

1087
00:55:37,060 --> 00:55:40,040
and they ran against Joe Biden and Kamala Harris.

1088
00:55:40,040 --> 00:55:42,440
So this is again a different parallel universe.

1089
00:55:42,440 --> 00:55:44,120
So the model will take educated guesses

1090
00:55:44,120 --> 00:55:45,680
and it will continue the token sequence

1091
00:55:45,680 --> 00:55:46,800
based on this knowledge.

1092
00:55:47,880 --> 00:55:50,160
And it will just kind of like all we're seeing here

1093
00:55:50,160 --> 00:55:51,960
is what's called hallucination.

1094
00:55:51,960 --> 00:55:54,960
The model is just taking its best guess

1095
00:55:54,960 --> 00:55:56,480
in a probabilistic manner.

1096
00:55:56,480 --> 00:55:57,680
The next thing I would like to show you

1097
00:55:57,680 --> 00:55:59,560
is that even though this is a base model

1098
00:55:59,560 --> 00:56:01,480
and not yet an assistant model,

1099
00:56:01,480 --> 00:56:04,160
it can still be utilized in particle applications.

1100
00:56:04,160 --> 00:56:06,680
If you are clever with your prompt design.

1101
00:56:06,680 --> 00:56:10,580
So here's something that we would call a few shot prompt.

1102
00:56:10,580 --> 00:56:14,940
So what it is here is that I have 10 words or 10 pairs.

1103
00:56:14,940 --> 00:56:18,900
And each pair is a word of English, colon,

1104
00:56:18,900 --> 00:56:21,660
and then the translation in Korean.

1105
00:56:21,660 --> 00:56:23,730
And we have 10 of them.

1106
00:56:23,730 --> 00:56:25,770
And what the model does here is at the end,

1107
00:56:25,770 --> 00:56:27,090
we have teacher, colon,

1108
00:56:27,090 --> 00:56:29,250
And then here's where we're gonna do a completion

1109
00:56:29,250 --> 00:56:31,480
of say just fact tokens.

1110
00:56:31,480 --> 00:56:33,280
And these models have what we call

1111
00:56:33,280 --> 00:56:35,440
in-context learning abilities.

1112
00:56:35,440 --> 00:56:36,960
And what that's referring to is that

1113
00:56:36,960 --> 00:56:39,400
as it is reading this context,

1114
00:56:39,400 --> 00:56:42,760
it is learning sort of in place

1115
00:56:42,760 --> 00:56:44,840
that there's some kind of an algorithmic pattern

1116
00:56:44,840 --> 00:56:46,520
going on in my data.

1117
00:56:46,520 --> 00:56:48,840
And it knows to continue that pattern.

1118
00:56:48,840 --> 00:56:51,830
And this is called kind of like in-context learning.

1119
00:56:51,830 --> 00:56:55,910
So it takes on the role of a translator

1120
00:56:55,910 --> 00:56:58,150
and when we hit completion,

1121
00:56:58,150 --> 00:56:59,950
we see that the teacher translation is

1122
00:56:59,950 --> 00:57:01,390
Sonsanim, which is correct.

1123
00:57:02,710 --> 00:57:04,470
And so this is how you can build apps

1124
00:57:04,470 --> 00:57:06,190
by being clever with your prompting,

1125
00:57:06,190 --> 00:57:08,390
even though we still just have a base model for now.

1126
00:57:08,390 --> 00:57:10,070
And it relies on what we call this

1127
00:57:11,470 --> 00:57:12,950
in context learning ability

1128
00:57:12,950 --> 00:57:15,350
and it is done by constructing what's called

1129
00:57:15,350 --> 00:57:16,870
a few shot prompt.

1130
00:57:16,870 --> 00:57:17,710
Okay, and finally,

1131
00:57:17,710 --> 00:57:19,270
I want to show you that there is a clever way

1132
00:57:19,270 --> 00:57:22,390
to actually instantiate a whole language model

1133
00:57:22,390 --> 00:57:24,750
assistant just by prompting.

1134
00:57:24,750 --> 00:57:27,590
And the trick to it is that we're gonna structure a prompt

1135
00:57:27,590 --> 00:57:30,310
to look like a web page that is a conversation

1136
00:57:30,310 --> 00:57:33,710
between a helpful AI assistant and a human.

1137
00:57:33,710 --> 00:57:36,310
And then the model will continue that conversation.

1138
00:57:36,310 --> 00:57:37,830
So actually, to write the prompt,

1139
00:57:37,830 --> 00:57:41,550
I turned to chat GPT itself, which is kind of meta.

1140
00:57:41,550 --> 00:57:44,030
But I told it, I want to create an all in assistant,

1141
00:57:44,030 --> 00:57:46,150
but all I have is the base model.

1142
00:57:46,150 --> 00:57:51,050
So can you please write my prompt?

1143
00:57:51,050 --> 00:57:52,330
And this is what it came up with,

1144
00:57:52,330 --> 00:57:53,330
which is actually quite good.

1145
00:57:53,330 --> 00:57:56,730
So here's a conversation between an AI assistant and a human.

1146
00:57:56,730 --> 00:57:58,650
The AI assistant is knowledgeable, helpful,

1147
00:57:58,650 --> 00:58:02,110
capable of answering wider IT questions, et cetera.

1148
00:58:02,110 --> 00:58:06,750
And then here, it's not enough to just give it a sort of description.

1149
00:58:06,750 --> 00:58:09,310
It works much better if you create this few shot prompt.

1150
00:58:09,310 --> 00:58:13,930
So here's a few terms of human assistant, human assistant.

1151
00:58:13,930 --> 00:58:17,530
And we have a few terms of conversation.

1152
00:58:17,530 --> 00:58:20,890
And then here at the end is we're going to be putting the actual query that we like.

1153
00:58:20,890 --> 00:58:25,280
So let me copy paste this into the base model prompt.

1154
00:58:25,280 --> 00:58:28,680
And now, let me do a human column.

1155
00:58:28,680 --> 00:58:30,840
And this is where we put our actual prompt.

1156
00:58:30,840 --> 00:58:33,760
Why is this sky blue?

1157
00:58:33,760 --> 00:58:39,080
And let's run assistant.

1158
00:58:39,080 --> 00:58:40,800
The sky appears blue due to the phenomenon

1159
00:58:40,800 --> 00:58:43,160
called Raylite scattering, et cetera, et cetera.

1160
00:58:43,160 --> 00:58:44,720
So you see that the base model is just

1161
00:58:44,720 --> 00:58:46,280
continuing the sequence.

1162
00:58:46,280 --> 00:58:49,200
But because the sequence looks like this conversation,

1163
00:58:49,200 --> 00:58:50,840
it takes on that role.

1164
00:58:50,840 --> 00:58:53,960
But it is a little subtle because here it just, you know,

1165
00:58:53,960 --> 00:58:55,840
it ends the assistant and then just, you know,

1166
00:58:55,840 --> 00:58:57,960
hallucinates the next question by the human, et cetera.

1167
00:58:57,960 --> 00:59:00,120
So we'll just continue going on and on.

1168
00:59:00,120 --> 00:59:03,840
But you can see that we have a sort of accomplished a task.

1169
00:59:03,840 --> 00:59:07,080
And if you just took this, why is this kind of blue?

1170
00:59:07,080 --> 00:59:10,080
And if we just refresh this and put it here,

1171
00:59:10,080 --> 00:59:11,440
then of course, we don't expect this to work

1172
00:59:11,440 --> 00:59:12,440
with a base model, right?

1173
00:59:12,440 --> 00:59:14,880
We're just going to, who knows what we're going to get?

1174
00:59:14,880 --> 00:59:17,250
Okay, we're just going to get more questions.

1175
00:59:17,250 --> 00:59:20,570
Okay, so this is one way to create an assistant,

1176
00:59:20,570 --> 00:59:23,490
even though you may only have a base model.

1177
00:59:23,490 --> 00:59:25,410
Okay, so this is the kind of brief summary

1178
00:59:25,410 --> 00:59:29,450
of the things we talked about over the last few minutes.

1179
00:59:29,450 --> 00:59:33,770
Now, let me zoom out here,

1180
00:59:33,770 --> 00:59:36,320
and this is kind of like what we've talked about so far.

1181
00:59:36,320 --> 00:59:39,840
We wish to train alarm assistants like ChachyBT.

1182
00:59:39,840 --> 00:59:41,920
We've discussed the first stage of that,

1183
00:59:41,920 --> 00:59:43,360
which is the pre-training stage,

1184
00:59:43,360 --> 00:59:44,920
and we saw that really what it comes down to

1185
00:59:44,920 --> 00:59:46,440
is we take internet documents,

1186
00:59:46,440 --> 00:59:48,040
we break them up into these tokens,

1187
00:59:48,040 --> 00:59:50,320
these atoms of little text chunks,

1188
00:59:50,320 --> 00:59:54,040
and then we predict token sequences using neural networks.

1189
00:59:54,040 --> 00:59:57,280
The output of this entire stage is this base model.

1190
00:59:57,280 --> 01:00:00,740
It is the setting of the parameters of this network.

1191
01:00:00,740 --> 01:00:02,340
And this base model is basically

1192
01:00:02,340 --> 01:00:05,220
an internet document simulator on the token level.

1193
01:00:05,220 --> 01:00:08,480
So it can just, it can generate token sequences

1194
01:00:08,480 --> 01:00:10,740
that have the same kind of like statistics

1195
01:00:10,740 --> 01:00:12,580
as internet documents.

1196
01:00:12,580 --> 01:00:14,280
And we saw that we can use it in some applications,

1197
01:00:14,280 --> 01:00:15,700
but we actually need to do better.

1198
01:00:15,700 --> 01:00:17,020
We want an assistant.

1199
01:00:17,020 --> 01:00:18,300
We want to be able to ask questions

1200
01:00:18,300 --> 01:00:20,620
and we want the model to give us answers.

1201
01:00:20,620 --> 01:00:23,140
And so we need to now go into the second stage,

1202
01:00:23,140 --> 01:00:25,900
which is called the post training stage.

1203
01:00:25,900 --> 01:00:29,020
So we take our base model, our internal document simulator,

1204
01:00:29,020 --> 01:00:31,340
and hand it off to post-training.

1205
01:00:31,340 --> 01:00:32,980
So we're now going to discuss a few ways

1206
01:00:32,980 --> 01:00:36,220
to do what's called post-training of these models.

1207
01:00:36,220 --> 01:00:37,700
These stages in post-training are

1208
01:00:37,700 --> 01:00:40,140
going to be computationally much less expensive,

1209
01:00:40,140 --> 01:00:43,020
most of the computational work, all of the massive data

1210
01:00:43,020 --> 01:00:48,500
centers, and all of the heavy compute and millions of dollars

1211
01:00:48,500 --> 01:00:50,460
are the pre-training stage.

1212
01:00:50,460 --> 01:00:52,700
But now we're going to the slightly cheaper,

1213
01:00:52,700 --> 01:00:55,580
but still extremely important stage called post-training,

1214
01:00:55,580 --> 01:00:58,980
where we turn this LLM model into an assistant.

1215
01:00:58,980 --> 01:01:01,660
So let's take a look at how we can get our model

1216
01:01:01,660 --> 01:01:04,100
to not sample internet documents,

1217
01:01:04,100 --> 01:01:06,970
but to give answers to questions.

1218
01:01:06,970 --> 01:01:08,210
So in other words, what we want to do

1219
01:01:08,210 --> 01:01:10,730
is we want to start thinking about conversations.

1220
01:01:10,730 --> 01:01:13,010
And these are conversations that can be multi-turned,

1221
01:01:13,010 --> 01:01:15,730
so that we multiple turns.

1222
01:01:15,730 --> 01:01:17,250
And they are, in the simplest case,

1223
01:01:17,250 --> 01:01:20,330
a conversation between a human and an assistant.

1224
01:01:20,330 --> 01:01:21,530
And so for example, we can imagine

1225
01:01:21,530 --> 01:01:23,410
that the conversation could look something like this.

1226
01:01:23,410 --> 01:01:25,250
When a human says what is 2 plus 2,

1227
01:01:25,250 --> 01:01:28,290
the assistant should respond with something like 2 plus 2 is 4.

1228
01:01:28,290 --> 01:01:31,730
When a human follows up and says, what if it was star instead of a plus?

1229
01:01:31,730 --> 01:01:34,900
Assistant could respond with something like this.

1230
01:01:34,900 --> 01:01:37,940
And similar here, this is another example showing that the assistant could also have

1231
01:01:37,940 --> 01:01:41,700
some kind of a personality here that it's kind of like nice.

1232
01:01:41,700 --> 01:01:45,140
And then here on the third example, I'm showing that when a human is asking for something

1233
01:01:45,140 --> 01:01:49,420
that we don't wish to help with, we can produce what's called a refusal.

1234
01:01:49,420 --> 01:01:51,900
We can say that we cannot help with that.

1235
01:01:51,900 --> 01:01:55,180
So in other words, what we want to do now is we want to think through

1236
01:01:55,180 --> 01:01:57,820
how an assistant should interact with the human.

1237
01:01:57,820 --> 01:01:59,780
